<p>Australia has opened investigations into major social media platforms including Facebook, Instagram, Snapchat, TikTok and YouTube over suspected breaches of its ban on under-16s holding accounts, with the government and the eSafety Commission saying many children are still able to access restricted services three months after the law took effect in December. Officials said the law was introduced to protect minors from harmful online content and recommendation systems associated with sex, violence and other risks to well-being. The government says the measure itself remains intact but compliance by platforms is falling short, and penalties could be decided by mid-2026 if regulators find systemic failures. The first public compliance assessment has also drawn international attention because Australia’s model is being studied by policymakers globally, suggesting the case could shape how democracies try to balance child safety, platform responsibility and open internet access.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/JOsUjS7K-f26ypKSsvf5Hwx8I7utwhfrxE1E7tyt-SQoeIfVPsipuBufjMCAsaqd14DPmT6XgIzdqGQhJMJOfxBBS8BzX6APEuG-1evlwaho' alt='Stella Pieterse, 14, poses holding a mobile phone displaying a message from TikTok as Australia’s under-16 social media law takes effect in Sydney on Dec. 10, 2025.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Accounts removed: More than five million accounts belonging to underage Australian users have been removed since the restrictions came into force, according to the eSafety Commission.</li><li>Possible penalties: Platforms found to have systemic breaches can face fines of up to A$49.5 million, equivalent to about $33.9 million.</li><li>Court action risk: Australia says it is considering taking some companies to court over allegations they are not doing enough to keep children younger than 16 off restricted platforms.</li></ul><blockquote>Australia’s world-leading social media laws are not failing. But big tech is failing to obey the laws. - Anika Wells</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Australian government: Communications Minister Anika Wells said the alleged breaches reflect a familiar &#x27;big tech playbook&#x27; and argued that billion-dollar platforms have no excuse for failing to comply with Australian law if they want to keep operating in the market. (<a href='https://michaelwest.com.au/probe-into-tech-giants-for-possible-age-ban-breach/'>Michael West</a>, <a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1987108/australia-investigates-tech-giants-over-social-media-ban-breaches'>Dawn</a>)</li><li>eSafety Commission: In its first compliance report since the ban began, the regulator said some services still did not provide appropriate pathways for suspected under-16 accounts to be reported, suggesting compliance problems extend beyond age checks alone. (<a href='https://michaelwest.com.au/probe-into-tech-giants-for-possible-age-ban-breach/'>Michael West</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.lemonde.fr/en/australia/article/2026/03/31/australia-investigates-tech-giants-over-breaches-of-social-media-ban-for-under-16s_6751973_223.html'>Australia investigates tech giants over breaches of social media ban for under-16s</a> - lemonde.fr</li><li><a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1987108/australia-investigates-tech-giants-over-social-media-ban-breaches'>Australia investigates tech giants over social media ban breaches</a> - dawn.com</li><li><a href='https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cy4181pkxl2o'>Social media firms must better enforce Australia under-16 ban, watchdog says</a> - bbc.com</li><li><a href='https://www.thehindu.com/sci-tech/technology/australia-investigates-tech-giants-over-social-media-ban-compliance/article70805599.ece'>Australia investigates tech giants over social media ban compliance</a> - thehindu.com</li><li><a href='https://abcnews.com/International/wireStory/meta-snapchat-tiktok-youtube-fully-complying-child-account-131565643'>Meta, TikTok and YouTube aren&#x27;t fully complying with account ban, Australia says</a> - abcnews.com</li><li><a href='https://michaelwest.com.au/probe-into-tech-giants-for-possible-age-ban-breach/'>Probe into tech giants for possible age ban breach</a> - michaelwest.com.au</li></ul>